OL,3,GNEWSAWoodlands project records more
OL,4,Atrees than expected
OL,5,
OL,6,FA project which aims to restore ancient
OL,7,Fwoodlands in Sussex has hit a
OL,8,Fsignificant "milestone" with over 5,500
OL,9,Ftrees now recorded.
OL,11,FSince March 2024, volunteers with the
OL,12,FLost Woods project have been recording
OL,13,Fancient, veteran and notable trees
OL,14,Facross a 121-sq-miles (314-sq-km) area
OL,15,Ffrom Lewes to Storrington.
OL,17,FAccording to the latest figures, they
OL,18,Fhave now recorded 5,570 trees, a number
OL,19,Fwhich Lost Woods outreach adviser, Bob
OL,20,FEpsom, said is more than "we expected"
OL,21,Fand signifies a "milestone" for the
OL,22,Fproject.
OL,23,APub: 03-OctQppppppppppppppppppppppG1/2
OL,24,ANext PageBLon/SE NewsCReg TVFClean Feed
